Go.dot patch 0002 for JUCE (Go.dot's own changes): an aggregate device with no clock device is not a crash.

AggregateAudioDevice::getClockingDeviceUid() asks CoreAudio for an aggregate
device's clock device and then its main sub-device, each a CFString, and took
the length of whichever came back. A device that has neither answers with no
string at all - getPropertyOrDefault's default, a null CFStringRef - and
CFStringGetLength of null is EXC_BAD_ACCESS at address nought. It is met in a
scan of the audio devices (CoreAudioIODeviceType::createDevice, which builds an
AggregateDeviceDescription for every name) when an aggregate is on the list
without its clock: one CoreAudio is still putting together or taking apart, as
JUCE's own are around a device session.

Found 2026-11-20 on GitHub's macOS runner, where tests/DeviceTests.cpp ended in
SIGSEGV at every push from 99600f50 on: a case plays through "Apple Virtual
Sound Device", and a later case's audio::availableDevices() crashed here. Read
off macOS's own crash report, printed by the CI job; the Mac mini never met it,
under AddressSanitizer either. In Go.dot the same scan is `wfg devices` and the
Show settings' audio page, so a crash there would have been the whole program.

The change is one test: no string is no name, as an empty one is, and the
function goes on to the sub-devices and then to "none", which its caller
already takes. Each hunk carries a notice that Go.dot modified the file (AGPL-3
and GPL-3, section 5(a)).

Not JUCE's own fix, which is why it is not in patches/JUCE: not checked against
JUCE's develop, and to be reported there. Applied on every platform so every
build tree has one state; the file compiles on macOS alone.

LINE ENDINGS. JUCE stores this file with CRLF, so the hunk's lines end in CRLF and
must stay so; .gitattributes keeps this file byte for byte (-text).

Applied by Go.dot's build, cmake/WfgJucePatches.cmake, in the order
patches/JUCE-local/series gives; refreshed from the working tree with
scripts/patches.py JUCE-local refresh, which keeps this header. Against JUCE
7ff644f65d7d (9.0.0+, the JUCE Tracktion Engine a5e4499 pins). Drop it when JUCE
tests the string itself.

